      "id": "7b3b878e-6fc1-4c2f-ab5d-fd8380c5aa17",
      "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
      "data": {
        "body": "\\textbf{Part A}:  Three students present scienti c arguments about  nding the speed v at which the block is launched into the air at\nthe top of the spring.  Which is/are correct?\n\nBeth: \\textbf{Claim}: I chose the block as the system and everything else as the surroundings.  \\textbf{Evidence}: Based on the situation\ndescribed in the problem, I was told only the block is interacting with the spring and Earth while on a ramp.  \\textbf{Reasoning}:\nTherefore, I would have a change in kinetic energy from the block, change in potential energy from Earth, work from the spring,\nand work from friction of the ramp\n\nCathy: \\textbf{Claim}: I chose the block and spring as the system and everything else as the surroundings. \\textbf{Evidence}:  Based on the\nsituation described in the problem, I was told only the block and spring are interacting with the Earth and the ramp. \\textbf{Reasoning}:\nTherefore, I would have a change in kinetic energy from the block, work from the spring, work from Earth, and work from friction\nof the ramp.\n\nAmy: \\textbf{Claim}: I chose the block, spring, and Earth in the system, and the ramp in the surroundings. \\textbf{Evidence}: Based on the\nsituation described in the problem, I was only about the block, spring, and Earth with the ramp not experiencing any change. \n\\textbf{Reasoning}: Therefore, I would have a change in kinetic energy from the block, change in potential energy from the spring, a\nchange in potential energy from Earth, and work from friction of the ramp",
        "solution": [
          3
        ],
        "answerChoices": [
          {
            "id": "97ff851c-2940-4cf2-8ff6-6a26c27036d9",
            "index": 0,
